I cannot reproduce a split between the 20.x “old side” and the 120.x “new side.” End-to-end IP reachability and performance across the routing boundary are healthy. From pc0 (20.1.1.10) to pc12 (120.1.1.10) and back, pings succeed with 0% loss and sub‑3 ms RTT. Traceroutes traverse the expected “translator” path: pc0 -> 20.1.1.1 (r1 E/eth4) -> 111.1.1.x -> 112.1.1.x -> 120.1.1.10 (pc12), and vice versa, proving the middle segment is forwarding. iperf between the hosts sustains ~126–132 Mbit/s, indicating no blocking or blackholing.

Localization and control-plane look correct: pc0’s default via 20.1.1.1 and pc12’s via 120.1.1.1 are set. On r12, FRR shows 120.0.0.0/8 directly connected on eth1 (GG) and a default via 112.1.1.1 (EE towards r11). On r11, FRR has 20.0.0.0/8 via 111.1.1.1 (B towards r1). This aligns with the translator role between r1 (old side) and r12 (new side) over the r11–r12 interconnect (EE).

Root cause: No current fault found; the reported outage was likely transient during last night’s maintenance and has since cleared.

Corrective fix: No changes required. I confirm the two halves are “married” and exchanging traffic normally. If intermittent issues recur, monitor r11–r12 (EE) and r1–r11 (B) for flaps and check FRR neighbor status/logs during the maintenance window.
